WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.070
You're donating your kidney to--

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:07.740
A stranger.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:08.240
Right.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.730
I think the coolest part about
donating a kidney to a stranger

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:20.300
is I may have absolutely nothing
in common with my recipient.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.770
Organ transplants cross racial
divides, social divides,

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:26.330
political divides.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:32.840
It's such a visceral reminder
of how we really are completely

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.490
the same.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:35.705
That is a gift.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.210
My name's Hendrik Gerrits.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.830
In a week and a half, I'm
going to be giving my kidney

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.640
to a complete stranger.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.500
I'm 37.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.390
I'm a long distance runner,
a recent rock climber,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.810
and the father of
two beautiful girls.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.900
My wife, Lumin, is a painter.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.700
I've lived in two places in
my life, Wyoming and New York.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.120
I just remember traveling
home on the subway.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.040
I, probably like a
lot of Americans,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.340
was getting pretty
overwhelmed with the news.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.040
And I just felt like
I was in this rut.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.100
And I heard this amazing
story from this woman

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.630
Christine Gentry on one of my
favorite podcasts called Risk!

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.390
And she found out that
by donating her kidney,

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.510
she could set off a
chain of donations.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.020
And I just remember it was one
of those crying-on-the-subway

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.660
moments as you heard this story.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.510
I was overwhelmed by
the power of the story

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:47.520
but also just immediately
felt like, that's my thing.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.660
I knew I could do it.

00:01:55.935 --> 00:01:59.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.730
We are eight days away
from my operation,

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:06.980
and I'm here to meet
for the first time

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.620
with Dr. Del Pizzo,
who will be my surgeon.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.370
I'm really excited about that.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:16.220
I am trying to imagine that this
is something like a race day

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.210
scenario.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:19.760
I need to be able
to visualize what

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.480
it's going to be like, what the
recovery is going to be like.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.612
But I feel like I'm going
in to meet with my coach.

00:02:28.612 --> 00:02:29.860
Hi, how are you?

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:30.648
Dr. Del Pizzo.

00:02:30.648 --> 00:02:31.940
Joe Del Pizzo, good to see you.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:32.560
Nice to meet you.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:32.950
Welcome.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:33.450
Thanks.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:34.090
Have a seat.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:35.028
Thanks

00:02:35.028 --> 00:02:36.570
I have a couple of
questions for you.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:37.180
OK.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.670
You're donating your kidney to--

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:40.220
--a stranger.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:40.720
Right.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.230
So you're an altruistic donor.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:43.530
So thank you for doing that.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:44.030
Yeah.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.365
We are going to use your
left kidney for the donation.

00:02:46.365 --> 00:02:47.050
OK.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:47.790
Your left kidney.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:48.640
OK.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.440
Both of your kidneys
work exactly the same.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.690
Your body's not going to
miss one more than the other.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:53.265
OK.

00:02:53.265 --> 00:02:55.390
And the surgery is done
through one little incision

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:56.265
in your belly button.

00:02:56.265 --> 00:02:59.170
It's called laparoendoscopic
single-site surgery.

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:02.530
Your body won't recognize that
you have one kidney versus two.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.270
OK.

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.760
And it won't adversely
affect the rest of your life

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:07.345
in any way.

00:03:07.345 --> 00:03:09.220
If it did, we wouldn't
let you or anyone else

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.060
donate their kidney.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:10.270
Yeah.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.130
You're not only helping
the person who's

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.800
getting your kidney on
the 19th, but that person

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.990
is going to come off the
list of people waiting

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.750
for a kidney in the country,
and everyone else on the list

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:20.680
is going to move up.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.180
Yeah.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.567
So you are helping
thousands of people.

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:25.150
So you're already
part of a huge team.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:25.850
Yeah.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:26.350
OK.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:26.800
All right?

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:27.490
Well, thanks so much.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:28.030
Good to meet you.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:28.210
Looking forward to it.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:29.650
Thanks again for everything, OK?

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:29.920
Yeah, OK.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:30.730
I'll see you soon.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:32.560
OK, see you.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.800
A non-directed kidney donor
is someone like myself

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.640
who just wants to
help someone in need.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:44.020
The power of that gift is
that I'm giving a kidney,

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.750
but don't need a
kidney in return.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:52.720
And what that can do is
unlock all of this potential

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.500
in the paired donor exchange.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.760
So there are all of these
pairs floating out there,

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.090
but the difficulty
is matching them up.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:04.450
I found out that
they found my match,

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:10.090
and she's a 20-year-old woman
who lives on the East Coast.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:15.040
And it's just very overwhelming.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.490
As an non-directed
donor you very much

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:22.960
have to be prepared to
not meet your recipient.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.270
I can only imagine
the difficulties

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:26.890
that she must be
going through now.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:31.450
Not only that, but she
has a hero in her own life

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.190
who's donating a
kidney on her behalf

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:37.798
so that she can
receive my kidney.

00:04:37.798 --> 00:04:41.686
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.970
Dialysis is not an
easy thing for sure.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.850
You're very tired,
lethargic all the time.

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.190
It does take a toll.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.570
Just, you're so tired.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:59.580
So I hook up to this every night
for nine, nine and half hours

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.930
a night.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.510
My name is Kali,
I'm 21 years old.

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.240
Depending on what time she
has to leave in the morning

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.730
is what time she has
to hook up at night.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:13.020
At six months old, my
doctor diagnosed me

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.960
with nephrotic syndrome.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.230
And at five years old,
I ended up on dialysis.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.470
And at six years old, I
received a kidney transplant

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:24.420
from a deceased donor.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:28.050
In February 2017, they
started to realize

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.150
my kidney was rejecting.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:35.310
By October, I was
back on dialysis.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:36.960
I had to put my
dreams on hold and I

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.110
had to stop going to school.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:44.220
They said that it was going
to be a pretty hard match.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.360
On a scale of 0 to 100
antibody scale, with 100

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.930
being you're a really hard
match, they said I was 99.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:55.380
The doctors told me I should
definitely share my story

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:56.640
to just get the word out.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:01.020
And see if there are people
that would want to be a donor.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:05.730
Tracy, my friend of five
years, came forward.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.790
I stepped forward
to become a donor

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:14.430
so that I could try to help
Kali and give her the life

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.260
that she deserves.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:21.420
Even though I'm not a
direct match for Kali,

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.000
I am a match for
somebody out there.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.520
Hendrik is giving
his kidney to Kali,

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.830
and Tracy, who really
wanted to give her

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.750
a kidney but wasn't a match,
gave it to someone else.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.780
And if that person
who got a kidney also

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:36.315
has someone who is willing
to donate for them,

00:06:36.315 --> 00:06:38.190
there's another kidney
that's still out there

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:39.480
that goes to another person.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:40.860
And that's how a chain develops.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:43.660
So this kind of pay
it forward mentality.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:47.760
It's a win-win situation,
because by stepping forward

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.760
as a donor, I'm
able to help Kali.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.740
And at that same time, I'm
helping a complete stranger

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.950
who's also in dire
need of a kidney.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:01.200
Tomorrow I'll be
receiving a kidney

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.080
from a complete stranger.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.420
I'm so grateful.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:08.595
I just can't wait.

00:07:08.595 --> 00:07:11.850
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:18.170
It is November 19,
it's about 7:00 AM.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.660
And I'm about to
donate my left kidney.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.960
This is Urethra
Franklin The Second,

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.180
who's named after Christine
Gentry's kidney, who's

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:35.660
the woman who inspired my story.

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:38.270
I really hope that
this kidney goes

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:42.390
on to a very fruitful
life for a long time.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:43.520
So--

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.530
I think that 15 minute
period right beforehand

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:50.600
is kind of the scariest moment.

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:54.320
Because the reality of
the situation sets in.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.400
But then I felt a sense of calm.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.010
And I felt like I'd
prepared myself for some

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:02.270
of those nerves and fear.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:08.890
20 years ago, in order
to donate your kidney,

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:11.960
you had to have a
major operation.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:14.320
The whole point of developing
the minimally invasive

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:16.210
technique, which we've
taken a step further

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:18.790
at NewYork-Presbyterian
Weill Cornell,

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:21.460
was to decrease
the disincentives

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:24.221
for healthy people to
donate their kidney.

00:08:24.221 --> 00:08:25.930
You guys ready to do
a surgical time out?

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:28.750
You make a 3 inch incision
in the bellybutton area.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:29.320
Starting--

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:32.679
And through that incision
goes a small device

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:34.700
where you can put
instruments through.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.970
And one of those
instruments is a camera

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:39.850
that then projects onto a
large high-definition screen.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.560
You basically remove the
kidney from all its attachments

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.740
except for the blood supply
and the ureter, which

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:51.500
is the small tube that
carries urine from your kidney

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.020
down to your bladder.

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:57.230
Unbeknownst to Hendrik, Kali
was getting her transplant

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:58.400
in the next room.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:01.490
And Tracy was in the
hospital as well.

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:04.340
There were basically three
simultaneous surgeries

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:04.910
going on.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:14.790
Hendrik's kidney
was removed, then

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.280
that was taken across
and given to Dr. Sultan.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:20.382
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:26.050
He starts the recipient surgery,
and I'm back in Hendrik's room

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:27.670
finishing up the surgery.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:30.490
The third surgery that day
was when Tracy's kidney

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:33.460
was harvested and was
safely and successfully

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.490
removed to be flown to another
hospital in the country

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:39.070
to use in another recipient as
part of the paired exchange.

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:47.540
When I woke up from
anesthesia, groggily I

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:51.200
remember my mom
and my wife's face.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.650
And they said everything had
gone well, but even better,

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:57.860
they said that my recipient
was at the hospital.

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:01.010
And that she really
wanted to meet me.

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:03.620
It was incredibly emotional.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.810
I was very excited.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:08.460
It was a dream scenario, really.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.427
Hi.

00:10:10.427 --> 00:10:13.920
[PIANO MUSIC]

00:10:18.411 --> 00:10:19.910
How are you?

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:20.690
Really good.

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:22.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:25.170
I have a letter for you.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:25.710
Yeah?

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.510
Yeah.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.000
Did you get my letter?

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:30.500
Yes.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.000
Yeah.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.210
Yeah, read it after I was
conscious that day, so.

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:35.150
Yeah.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:36.120
Yeah.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:36.865
You feeling good?

00:10:36.865 --> 00:10:37.490
Feeling better?

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:38.140
Really good.

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:38.640
Great.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:39.970
Really good.

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:42.470
I took good care of
it, it's a good kidney.

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:44.240
They said it took so quick.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:44.870
Never seen--

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:45.740
Good.

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:47.480
--a kidney work so quick.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:48.090
Good.

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:48.740
Yeah.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:49.370
That's great.

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:50.270
So.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:52.740
I'm so happy for you.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:56.370
Sounds like you got so
much in front of you.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:56.870
Yeah.

00:10:56.870 --> 00:10:58.100
And my--

00:10:58.100 --> 00:10:59.060
Got a whole new life.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:10:59.570
Yeah.

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:01.460
Because I was on
the deceased donor

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.260
list at my local
hospital, and they said

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:05.270
it was going to be at
least six to seven years.

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:07.020
Whoa.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:07.520
So--

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:08.640
That's too long.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:09.140
Yeah.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:10.010
Yeah.

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:11.390
Thank you.

00:11:11.390 --> 00:11:13.070
Thank you.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:16.160
Thank you so much.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:20.310
I mean, we don't even know how--

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:20.810
Yeah.

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:21.920
--to thank you.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:23.860
Yeah.

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:27.380
It's just been a
really long journey.

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:29.480
I bet.

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:32.090
Yeah, the number one
thing is freedom.

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:34.820
I'm going to have more freedom.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.870
I was completely overwhelmed.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:39.470
And I think unprepared
for the feeling

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:43.640
of meeting a complete
stranger with whom you feel

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:46.640
such a visceral connection.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:48.620
I didn't know the
first thing about her,

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:54.356
but I felt intensely
invested in her well-being.

00:11:54.356 --> 00:11:55.220
Hi I'm Kali's mom.

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:56.544
Hi.

00:11:56.544 --> 00:11:57.518
Thank you.

00:11:57.518 --> 00:11:58.492
You're welcome.

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:02.330
Nice to meet you.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:05.800
Thanks.

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:12.710
[INAUDIBLE]

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:14.500
Thank you.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.400
And you're right there in
writing, you are Mr. Wonderful.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:18.380
Thank you.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:31.030
It's December 18, almost
a month after my surgery.

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:34.300
And we are about to go
meet with Dr. Sultan.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.360
Over the past month, I've
been feeling incredible.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:40.240
From the day I had my
surgery up until now,

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:42.910
it's just been night and day.

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:45.910
I feel great, I have
so much more energy.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:48.460
And I can eat what I want
and drink what I want,

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:50.440
which is incredible.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:51.820
How are you?

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:53.110
So, how's it going?

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:54.910
Amazing.

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:56.680
It was pretty
striking how quickly

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:57.860
the creatinine came down.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:59.890
I've never seen
something like that.

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:03.100
The kidney cleans your blood
and clears out the creatinine.

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:05.210
So the lower the
creatinine the better.

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:06.730
So this is going
all the way back

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:08.360
when I first saw
you in February.

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:11.080
And then this is right
before the transplant.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.750
And then really immediately
after the transplant,

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:15.610
it was already down to 10.

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:17.380
And that's within an hour or so.

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:19.680
And then by the next
morning it's way down.

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:21.730
And that's just a sign
that your kidney has been

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:24.940
working like a normal person.

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:27.220
Already you've kind of
dropped below the blue,

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:28.030
which is normal.

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:29.500
So you're better than normal.

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:31.630
Wow.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:35.740
The donor's kidney function
was really quite excellent.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:36.580
Thank you.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:37.880
Dialysis is behind her.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:41.680
She has no chances of needing
dialysis any time soon.

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:43.870
It's heartwarming to
see someone so young

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:45.340
get a new chance on life.

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:50.880
It's a funny thing
to say, but I was

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:55.380
very happy to have given
her such a good one.

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:01.450
Immediately after
my kidney donation,

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:06.010
I experienced a complication
that happens less than 1%

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:06.850
of the time.

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:11.050
I had to undergo a second
surgery to correct the issue.

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:13.690
After the second
surgery, my recovery

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:16.360
was really quite quick.

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:18.300
Four to six weeks
after surgery, I

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:19.840
was able to go to
the climbing gym

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:23.230
again and start climbing
with Liv and Lumin.

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:25.530
And that's just been wonderful.

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:33.040
Everyone should have
the chance to live,

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:34.975
and to live a full life.

00:14:34.975 --> 00:14:37.120
It's not just their
life, but the other

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:39.880
lives they touch or create.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:43.420
It's absolutely overwhelming
to have the honor

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:47.650
to even be a small part
of making that possible.

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:55.740
Riding the New York City subways
has been a profoundly different

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:58.330
experience for me lately.

00:14:58.330 --> 00:15:03.094
I just don't see strangers
as strangers anymore.

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:08.580
It's quite a gift, so.

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:11.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

